"comparison": "Both Goldman Sachs and Bank of America disclose that the Stress Capital Buffer (SCB) is a component of their minimum CET1 capital ratio requirements, specifically applying only to the Standardized approach. GS states the FRB determines the SCB based on its own stress test calculation involving the difference between starting and minimum projected CET1 ratios under the supervisory severely adverse scenario and planned dividends. BAC similarly identifies the SCB as part of the requirements to avoid restrictions on capital distributions, noting it applies only under the Standardized approach (unlike the Advanced approach where it is not included). While GS focuses on the calculation mechanics and BAC focuses on the resulting threshold requirements (10.0% to 10.7%), both confirm the SCB's role in binding capital constraints for the Standardized approach.",
